5. FIREBASE AUTHENTICATION — SERVER AUTHORITY ONLY
======================================================================

This section is a critical security requirement. A model API key in a
secure backend is not enough if private business data can be requested
by spoofing a user ID or organization ID.

5.1 Verify Firebase ID tokens on the server

For every live-account AI request:
- require Authorization: Bearer <Firebase ID token>;
- reject missing, malformed, expired, revoked, or invalid tokens;
- call the existing Firebase Admin verifyBearerToken() helper or a
  hardened equivalent;
- derive uid, email, emailVerified, and other identity fields only from
  the verified token / Firebase Admin result;
- do not accept userId, userName, role, or admin status from the browser
  as authoritative;
- do not accept a fake demo profile as a real Firebase identity;
- do not allow anonymous live access to private organization records.

If the token is invalid, return 401 with a stable code such as
AUTH_REQUIRED or AUTH_TOKEN_INVALID. Do not call OpenRouter.

5.2 Verify organization membership separately from billing access

The existing verifyOrgBillingAccess helper is billing-focused and allows
only selected roles. Do not blindly reuse it as the Copilot's general
organization-membership function.

Create or use a dedicated server-side helper that:
- loads the organization document from the configured Firestore database;
- confirms the organization exists;
- checks organizations/{orgId}.ownerUid against the verified uid;
- otherwise reads organizations/{orgId}/members/{uid};
- confirms the membership exists and is active;
- derives the user's real organization role from the authoritative
  organization/member records;
- rejects suspended, removed, disabled, or inactive memberships;
- checks any additional role-specific data permissions required by the
  existing product architecture;
- returns only the authorized organization context.

Never trust a role string supplied in the request body.
Never infer organization membership from an organization ID alone.
Never treat platform-owner status as a user-provided boolean.

5.3 Tenant isolation

All live organization reads must be scoped to the verified organization
path, for example:

organizations/{verifiedOrgId}/projects/{documentId}
organizations/{verifiedOrgId}/requests/{documentId}
organizations/{verifiedOrgId}/incidents/{documentId}
organizations/{verifiedOrgId}/vendors/{documentId}
organizations/{verifiedOrgId}/goals/{documentId}

Use the actual existing collection names and data schema after inspecting
the repository. Do not assume every collection exists or has the same
fields.

Never query all organizations and filter only in browser code.
Never mix Tenant A and Tenant B data.
Never allow a user to submit an arbitrary orgSnapshot and have the server
accept it as authoritative live data.
Never let a client-provided orgId bypass membership verification.
Never put a Firestore Admin query outside a verified tenant scope.

The Admin SDK bypasses Firestore Security Rules. Therefore every server
Admin read must enforce identity, membership, active status, role, and
collection scope in server code before it queries tenant data.

5.4 Do not trust browser-supplied identity and permissions

For LIVE requests, remove or ignore these request-body fields as sources
of authority:
- userId;
- userName;
- userRole;
- permissionGranted;
- orgSnapshot;
- isAdmin;
- isPlatformOwner;
- isAuthenticated.

The browser may send a candidate orgId, but the server must verify it
against the authenticated user's actual membership.
The browser may send a demoMode flag only for the isolated synthetic
demo path. It cannot use demoMode to obtain live Firestore data.

5.5 User-specific data scope

A user's identity and tenant context must be resolved on every request.
Do not cache a verified identity or organization authorization across
unrelated users or reuse a prior user's snapshot for another request.
If you cache authorized data, cache keys must include the verified uid,
orgId, and any other relevant access-scope dimensions, and the cache must
have a short expiry and safe invalidation. Prefer no shared cache for
private tenant snapshots unless the current architecture already has a
verified tenant-safe cache implementation.

