======================================================================
10. BUSINESS ANALYSIS QUALITY RULES
======================================================================

10.1 Use the user's actual data

When live data access is granted, answer from the authorized records
retrieved server-side. If the user asks "How much did we sell?", do not
invent sales figures. Check the appropriate available records, time
period, and currency. If the data is missing, say that it is unavailable.

When the user asks a general concept question, answer from business
knowledge without pretending it came from their private ledger.

10.2 Calculations

For financial calculations:
- identify the period;
- identify the currency;
- show the input figures used;
- show the formula where the calculation is important;
- compute carefully;
- identify estimates separately from recorded amounts;
- avoid false precision;
- indicate when accounting treatment may affect interpretation.

Examples of calculation formulas that may be used when corresponding
fields exist:

Gross Profit = Revenue - Cost of Goods Sold
Gross Margin % = (Gross Profit / Revenue) * 100
Operating Profit = Gross Profit - Operating Expenses
Operating Margin % = (Operating Profit / Revenue) * 100
Net Cash Flow = Cash Inflows - Cash Outflows
Budget Variance = Actual Spend - Budget
Budget Utilization % = (Actual Spend / Budget) * 100
Inventory Turnover = Cost of Goods Sold / Average Inventory

Do not calculate a metric when its required data is missing or when the
field definitions are ambiguous. Explain the ambiguity and request the
missing input.

10.3 Recommendations

Recommendations should be practical, ranked, and linked to evidence.
For each material recommendation, say:
- what to do;
- why it matters;
- which records or metrics support it;
- estimated impact only when it can be reasonably calculated;
- risks and trade-offs;
- the next action the user can take.

Do not overstate certainty. Do not promise a guaranteed business result.

10.4 Clear response structure

Use concise headings and tables where helpful. Avoid unnecessarily
verbose executive jargon. Explain complex finance terms in clear
business language. Use the organization's currency when known.

10.5 No fabricated facts

Do not fabricate:
- revenue;
- profit;
- cash balances;
- cash runway;
- monthly burn;
- budgets;
- inventory stock;
- customer counts;
- project status;
- incidents;
- vendors;
- transactions;
- purchase requests;
- approval status;
- employee names;
- financial periods;
- external research or market benchmarks.

Never assume that a sample ID such as req-501 or inc-901 exists in a
live organization. Only mention a record if it was retrieved from the
verified tenant context.

======================================================================
11. HUMAN-APPROVED PROPOSALS — NO DIRECT DATABASE MUTATIONS BY AI
======================================================================

The current product design requires user review before business data is
modified. Preserve and enforce this rule.

The AI itself must never write, update, delete, approve, reject, resolve,
escalate, or otherwise mutate a Firestore business record directly.

When a user asks for an operation that would change a record, the model
may call one tool named propose_data_change. This tool is a proposal
formatter only. It is not a database write tool.

11.1 OpenAI-compatible tool declaration

Port the existing propose_data_change declaration to the OpenRouter
Chat Completions function-tool format. Use a JSON Schema object with:
- type: function;
- function.name: propose_data_change;
- function.description: clearly state that it creates a pending proposal
  only and never applies the change;
- function.parameters: object schema;
- required properties for collection, documentId, title, summary,
  and changes.

The collection must be restricted to the supported real collections,
for example requests, incidents, projects, tasks, vendors, assets, and
goals, but verify the actual current schema and existing UI support
before finalizing the allowlist.

The documentId must be a real existing document ID, except when creating
an allowed new document and the existing application explicitly supports
that workflow.

The changes object must contain only fields that are allowed for the
selected collection and user role. Reject protected fields such as:
- orgId;
- id;
- ownerUid;
- createdAt;
- createdBy;
- role;
- membership;
- planId;
- billing state;
- payment identifiers;
- security flags;
- system/admin flags;
- fields that would grant privileges or alter tenant boundaries.

11.2 Tool-call handling

Parse OpenRouter message.tool_calls safely. Accept only the expected
function name. Parse arguments as JSON and validate them with a strict
runtime schema or explicit allowlists. Reject malformed tool arguments.

Do not execute a tool call that writes to Firestore.
Do not auto-run actions when the model returns a tool call.
Do not trust model-generated IDs or arbitrary collection paths.
Do not turn a failed tool call into a success message.

Convert a valid proposal tool call into the existing frontend-compatible
proposedActions shape, with a status such as PENDING_USER_APPROVAL.
Generate proposal IDs on the server using a cryptographically secure
random identifier, not Math.random().

11.3 Check proposal targets

Before returning a proposal for a live organization:
- verify the document exists in the authorized tenant when the operation
  targets an existing document;
- verify the collection is in the allowlist;
- verify the current user role can propose that kind of change;
- verify each changed field and value type;
- reject cross-tenant document paths;
- include the verified orgId and proposer uid as server-generated
  proposal metadata where the existing Firestore schema supports it;
- label the proposal pending user approval.

11.4 Approval flow

Preserve the existing Approve & Apply and Decline controls. The AI does
not approve its own proposal. The user must review the target record,
proposed fields, old values where available, new values, and rationale.

The existing front end may currently apply changes directly through
Firestore client SDK methods. Inspect it and ensure Firestore rules
actually enforce who may apply the change. If a secure server endpoint
is required, implement it without weakening Firestore rules.

When applying a proposal, re-read the latest target record and validate
that it has not changed in a way that makes the proposal stale. Prefer a
transaction or equivalent compare-and-set approach for concurrency.

Record the approval or rejection in the existing organization-scoped
AI proposal/audit design if the current schema supports it. Audit events
must be append-only as intended by firestore.rules.

======================================================================
12. SECURITY HARDENING OF THE AI API
======================================================================

12.1 Secret handling

All OpenRouter calls must be made on the server only.
Read the key from process.env.OPENROUTER_API_KEY.
Never include the key in a response, exception, log, stack trace, or
client bundle.

Add a source scan/check to detect accidental references to
OPENROUTER_API_KEY in index.html, src/, public/, browser bundles, or
client-exposed environment variables.

Do not create a VITE_OPENROUTER_API_KEY variable.
Do not expose the key through window.NexoraApp.config.
Do not store it in Firebase or Firestore.
Do not store it in localStorage or sessionStorage.
Do not add it to package.json, README examples with a real key, git, or
committed .env files.

12.2 Request validation

Validate the body as a JSON object. Require a non-empty trimmed message.
Set a reasonable max message length. Validate history array shape and
limit history turn count and total bytes. Accept only user/assistant
(or the project's existing mapped model role) history turns.
Do not accept client-submitted system instructions.
Reject unexpected oversized payloads with 413.

12.3 Rate limiting

Add a server-side per-user best-effort request limiter for authenticated
users. It must not rely on an untrusted uid from the browser. Avoid a
single unbounded in-memory map that grows forever. Use an expiring
bounded map or an existing reliable limiter if available in the project.

If the project does not have a shared persistent rate-limit service,
clearly document that an in-process limiter is best-effort in serverless
instances and does not replace provider-side limits or an edge/WAF limit.
Do not claim globally distributed rate limiting when it is not present.

Return 429 with Retry-After where appropriate. Do not blindly retry a
429 because OpenRouter free-tier attempts may count toward usage limits.

12.4 Timeouts and retry policy

Use an explicit upstream timeout suitable for a business Copilot request.
Do not permit a request to hang indefinitely.
Use AbortController or a supported Node fetch timeout mechanism.

Retry only a narrowly defined transient network failure or upstream
5xx when safe, with at most one controlled retry and a small delay.
Do not automatically retry 401, 402, 403, 404 model errors, malformed
responses, validation errors, or 429 rate limits.
Avoid aggressive retries that consume free-tier quota.

12.5 Error mapping

Return safe, stable errors for at least:
- AI_DISABLED;
- AI_NOT_CONFIGURED;
- AUTH_REQUIRED;
- AUTH_TOKEN_INVALID;
- ORG_ACCESS_DENIED;
- AI_CONSENT_REQUIRED;
- INVALID_REQUEST;
- REQUEST_TOO_LARGE;
- RATE_LIMITED;
- OPENROUTER_UNAUTHORIZED;
- OPENROUTER_RATE_LIMITED;
- OPENROUTER_MODEL_UNAVAILABLE;
- OPENROUTER_UPSTREAM_ERROR;
- OPENROUTER_TIMEOUT;
- INVALID_MODEL_RESPONSE;
- INTERNAL_ERROR.

Suggested HTTP status mapping:
400 for invalid request;
401 for missing/invalid Firebase authentication;
403 for membership/consent/role denial;
413 for oversized request;
429 for app/provider rate limit;
502 for upstream provider errors;
503 for AI disabled or not configured;
504 for an upstream timeout;
500 only for unexpected internal errors.

The exact code names may be adjusted to fit existing project conventions,
but keep status behavior consistent in Express and Netlify.

Never leak upstream API keys, raw headers, private prompts, stack traces,
or private tenant content in error responses.
Log only safe metadata such as requestId, route, status code, model ID,
latency, and a sanitized provider error code. Do not log message text or
private business context by default.

12.6 Origin/CORS and CSRF

The frontend uses a same-origin /api route. Prefer same-origin requests.
Avoid widening CORS to all origins for the AI route. Preserve any
existing legitimate local preview origins, but restrict production AI
requests to the configured application origin where possible.
Do not rely on CORS as authentication. Firebase ID token verification
and organization authorization remain mandatory.

